WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.740
Here is an imagined human gene with four exons and three introns.

2
00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:15.920
From the start of transcription to the end of its last exon, it runs 5,137

3
00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:22.380
nucleotides. The finished mRNA carries only 737 of them.

4
00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:24.060
Where did the rest go?

5
00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:32.160
RNA polymerase II makes a primary transcript, and a primary transcript is not yet a message.

6
00:00:32.333 --> 00:00:35.253
Three changes turn it into a message.

7
00:00:36.253 --> 00:00:42.733
First, a cap goes on the 5′ end, once about 25 to 30 nucleotides have emerged.

8
00:00:43.473 --> 00:00:48.873
The cap is a methylated guanosine, joined by three phosphates, and it blocks

9
00:00:48.873 --> 00:00:56.893
exonucleases. Near the other end, the signal AAUAAA guides a cut in the

10
00:00:56.893 --> 00:01:05.693
RNA. Then poly(A) polymerase adds adenosines without copying any DNA, so the tail is not in the gene.

11
00:01:06.367 --> 00:01:12.327
Most human introns of the major class follow the GU-AG rule.

12
00:01:13.387 --> 00:01:18.827
GU sits at the 5′ end of the intron, and AG at the 3′ end.

13
00:01:19.567 --> 00:01:24.547
A third site inside the intron, the branch point, contains an adenosine.

14
00:01:25.387 --> 00:01:31.147
A small nuclear particle called U1 finds the 5′ splice site by base pairing.

15
00:01:32.107 --> 00:01:36.727
U2 pairs with the branch point and leaves the branch A bulged out.

16
00:01:37.517 --> 00:01:39.657
Splicing is two steps.

17
00:01:40.477 --> 00:01:46.217
In the first, the branch adenosine attacks the phosphate at the 5′ splice site.

18
00:01:46.857 --> 00:01:52.657
The upstream exon is freed, and the intron forms a loop with a tail, the lariat.

19
00:01:53.537 --> 00:01:58.997
In the second step, the freed upstream exon attacks the 3′ splice site.

20
00:01:59.637 --> 00:02:02.337
The exons are joined, and the lariat is released.

21
00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:05.860
Run it on the whole gene.

22
00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:13.820
Splicing removes intron 1, intron 2 and intron 3, and joins the four exons.

23
00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:21.380
The three introns add up to 4,400 nucleotides, and the exons add up to 737.

24
00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:26.000
The mRNA is shorter because the introns are gone.

25
00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:30.660
It also carries a cap and a tail that the gene never encoded.

26
00:02:31.550 --> 00:02:37.190
Change one base at a splice site, and the exon beside it is often skipped.

27
00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:43.730
Lose exon 2, and 87 nucleotides go, which is 3 × 29.

28
00:02:44.470 --> 00:02:49.070
The frame is kept, and the protein loses 29 amino acids.

29
00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:58.470
Lose exon 3 instead, and 100 go, 3 × 33 + 1, so the frame shifts by one base.

30
00:02:58.470 --> 00:03:05.070
Keep this rule: the frame survives only if the length lost is a multiple of 3.
